1968 0 obj
<</Type/Annot/Subtype/Highlight/C[.45 .6 .95]/P 1000 0 R/F 4/QuadPoints[473.15739 402.1324 479.9194 402.1324 473.15739 387.599 479.9194 387.599 117.6378 387.98118 234.59983 387.98118 117.6378 373.44779 234.59983 373.44779]/Rect[114.212139 372.53947 483.34507 403.0407]/AP<</N 1969 0 R>>/NM(fitz-A4)/Contents(REAL-FS-01: Getting real recordings onto the paper's 4 MHz grid [Paper silent, our choice, impact medium]\nWe do: realdata.py resamples every recording to 4 MHz with scipy's resample_poly and its default anti-alias filter: OAKBAT from 5 MHz \(ratio 4/5\), TEXBAT from 25 MHz \(ratio 4/25\). It was built for M1 and has never been checked as a faithful step.\nIf we got it wrong: A filter that is too narrow, or that rings, would widen or distort the correlation peak and move Pd in every real-data number. The bandwidth TEXBAT loses also caps what any 4 MHz method can see there, which matters when we compare against methods that run at 25 MHz.\nTo close: Test: on a clean OAKBAT segment and a clean TEXBAT segment, compare the CAF peak after resampling with a CAF formed at the native rate and decimated after correlation. Report the peak width and the peak-to-floor ratio, and state the filter in the write-up.)/T(REAL-FS-01)/BM/Multiply>>
endobj

