1980 0 obj
<</Type/Annot/Subtype/Highlight/C[.95 .55 .15]/P 1000 0 R/F 4/QuadPoints[181.86518 232.3324 279.48298 232.3324 181.86518 217.79901 279.48298 217.79901]/Rect[178.43952 216.89067 282.90864 233.24074]/AP<</N 1981 0 R>>/NM(fitz-A10)/Contents(EVAL-TESTSET-01: How big the test set is, and what one test image is [Deviates, impact medium]\nWe do: No fixed test set. For each C/N0 value, 700 fresh spoofed scenarios, each simulated six times \(K = 6\), scored at three tiles each: 1400 positives \(real and spoofer peak\) and 700 noise negatives. config.yaml's n_test_per_class: 1500 is never read; run.py hard-codes 700. Our spec \(section 10\) says the paper tests on "a ~3000-image test set per C/N0"; the journal says 3000 in total.\nIf we got it wrong: Measured from Fig. 8a: each Pfa step is 0.0053, so each journal Pfa is counted over about 189 noise cases per C/N0. Their curves therefore cannot go below Pfa 0.005, and a published Pfa of 0.05 carries a sampling error of about 0.016 on its own. Our digitized targets treat the points as exact up to reading error \(0.03 in Pd\). 3000 images over four C/N0 values is 750 per value, and 189 is about a quarter of that, which fits several splits and settles none. The Sensors acquisition figure steps in 1/697, a different test set again.\nTo close: Fix: carry the published sample size into the comparison as part of each target's tolerance \(binomial error from about 189 noise cases, and from the unknown number of positives\), instead of treating digitized points as exact. The step size is measured twice \(check:fig8a_two_pass\): exactly 1/189. The journal's '3000 images for testing' was added at revision, so it does not give the test size.)/T(EVAL-TESTSET-01)/BM/Multiply>>
endobj

