<</Type/Annot/Subtype/Highlight/C[.45 .6 .95]/P 1000 0 R/F 4/QuadPoints[194.474 218.1836 318.12449 218.1836 194.474 203.6502 318.12449 203.6502]/Rect[191.04834 202.74187 321.55015 219.09194]/AP<</N 1989 0 R>>/NM(fitz-A14)/Contents(SIG-CN0RANGE-01: Signal strength range used for training [Verified]\nWe do: Uniform 36 to 45 dB-Hz in training; scored at 36, 39, 42, 45.\n\nSIG-CN0DEF-01: What "C/N0 = 36 dB-Hz" means in the simulator [Paper silent, our choice, impact high]\nWe do: The textbook convention. Signal amplitude squared = C/N0 / fs with noise variance 1, so after 1 ms of correlation the peak stands C/N0 x 1 ms above the noise \(6 dB at 36 dB-Hz\).\nIf we got it wrong: The prime suspect for the strength gap. Our detector looks several dB stronger than theirs at the same C/N0. Gate A put it at 6 dB, but that figure compared different false-alarm rates \(RES-FIG7-36-01\); a matched reading of the Sensors ROC suggests about 4 to 5 dB at 36 dB-Hz \(RES-SENSORS6-01\). Common bookkeeping slips are worth exactly 3 dB \(noise variance per rail instead of total\) or 6 dB \(two such slips, or amplitude used where power was meant\). If this is the cause, the reconstruction is fine and only the axis label differs.\nTo close: Test it with the paper's own theory equations \(CAF-STDDET-02\), which leave this convention as the one free parameter. Candidates: ours \(amplitude^2 = \(C/N0\)/fs, unit noise variance\) and the group's convention in Li 2020 and Li 2023 \(amplitude sqrt\(2C\), per-rail noise sigma^2 = N0 B_Rx\) with B_Rx = fs or fs/2; the second makes their signal 3 dB stronger than ours, which would widen the strength gap. A second, cheaper route: digitize the noise floor of Fig. 1 and compare the peak-to-floor ratio with C/N0 x 1 ms.)/T(SIG-CN0RANGE-01 + SIG-CN0DEF-01)/BM/Multiply>>
endobj

