2024 0 obj
<</Type/Annot/Subtype/Highlight/C[.45 .6 .95]/P 396 0 R/F 4/QuadPoints[355.2056 196.14307 477.6507 196.14307 355.2056 181.60968 477.6507 181.60968]/Rect[351.77995 180.70134 481.07637 197.0514]/AP<</N 2025 0 R>>/NM(fitz-A4)/Contents(SIG-SPOOFPWR-01: How strong the spoofer is [Paper silent, our choice, impact medium]\nWe do: When scoring the detector, the spoofer is 1 dB weaker than the real signal \(run.py, run_fullscan.py\). The Fig. 9 counting run uses equal power instead \(run_phase2.py:84\).\nIf we got it wrong: Moves the spoofer half of our detection numbers. If their spoofer was weaker than ours, their curves sit lower, which is the direction of the gap. If stronger, the gap widens. Unknown either way.\nTo close: Reading: search the thesis for a spoofer power setting. If silent, sweep the spoofer power \(for example -6, -3, 0, +3 dB\) at scoring time. Training uses no spoofer, so no retraining is needed.\n\nSIG-SPOOFSEP-01: How far the spoofer sits from the real signal [Paper silent, our choice, impact medium]\nWe do: 60 to 320 samples apart in delay \(about 15 to 80 chips\) and 400 to 800 Hz apart in Doppler, random sign.\nIf we got it wrong: Small for the per-tile detector, because our offsets keep the two peaks in different tiles \(a tile is only 36 samples, about 9 chips, wide\). Large for the counting stage, which fails exactly when peaks overlap \(Fig. 9\).\nTo close: Reading: check the thesis for the offsets used in Figs. 7 and 8.)/T(SIG-SPOOFPWR-01 + SIG-SPOOFSEP-01)/BM/Multiply>>
endobj

