2048 0 obj
<</Type/Annot/Subtype/Highlight/C[.95 .55 .15]/P 1066 0 R/F 4/QuadPoints[357.56248 463.83818 477.6732 463.83818 357.56248 449.30479 477.6732 449.30479]/Rect[354.1368 448.39646 481.09886 464.7465]/AP<</N 2049 0 R>>/NM(fitz-A10)/Contents(TRAIN-LABELS-01: What the network is trained to tell apart [Deviates, impact high]\nWe do: Per-tile peak against noise. H1 is a tile with a centred peak \(real or spoofer\), H0 a tile of noise; the spoofer never appears in training. This is the Sensors acquisition task. 0053's spec \(decision D1\) adopted it from the false-alarm wording "the NN detects the noise as a signal/spoofer" \(journal p16, thesis p64\) and asked Hamza to confirm the choice; I found no record either way.\nIf we got it wrong: Every Gate A number would measure a different task from theirs. For our reading: probability maps and GMM peak counting only make sense if the network marks peaks, and the false-alarm wording treats signal and spoofer as one class. Against it: the training paragraph and Fig. 2. If they trained spoofed against clean tiles, their per-tile scores would be lower than ours at the same C/N0, the direction of the strength gap.\nTo close: Experiment, now required: train the spoofed-against-clean reading \(H1 = centred tiles from spoofed snapshots, H0 = centred real-peak tiles from clean snapshots\) and score both readings against the published curves. Gate A's detector verdict must then say which task it reproduced.)/T(TRAIN-LABELS-01)/BM/Multiply>>
endobj

