2056 0 obj
<</Type/Annot/Subtype/Highlight/C[.98 .82 .2]/P 1066 0 R/F 4/QuadPoints[207.99289 175.46558 327.80586 175.46558 207.99289 160.93219 327.80586 160.93219]/Rect[204.56723 160.02385 331.2315 176.37392]/AP<</N 2057 0 R>>/NM(fitz-A14)/Contents(REAL-SNAPSHOTS-01: Six consecutive real milliseconds: phase, data bits and drift come with the data [Implemented, unverified, impact medium]\nWe do: real_caf_snapshots cuts six consecutive 1 ms blocks from the recording, starting at an arbitrary sample, with one coarse Doppler for all six. So real snapshots carry a continuous carrier phase, a data-bit edge inside about 1 in 20 snapshots \(a sign change in about half of those\), and code-delay drift that stays under 0.02 chip over 6 ms. The synthetic faithful arm has none of these for Figs. 7 and 8: random phase in every snapshot, no bits, no drift.\nIf we got it wrong: If the synthetic arm's random-phase policy is not what a real receiver sees, part of any synthetic-to-real gap may be a phase-policy difference rather than a difference in the data.\nTo close: Test: on clean real segments, measure the snapshot-to-snapshot phase of the correlation peak and count bit edges, and check that the coherent synthetic policy \(run_phase2.py\) matches what the data shows. If it does, use the coherent policy for any synthetic arm set beside real data.)/T(REAL-SNAPSHOTS-01)/BM/Multiply>>
endobj

