2108 0 obj
<</Type/Annot/Subtype/Highlight/C[.98 .82 .2]/P 1537 0 R/F 4/QuadPoints[117.635898 463.98939 274.59077 463.98939 117.635898 449.456 274.59077 449.456]/Rect[114.21023 448.54768 278.01643 464.8977]/AP<</N 2109 0 R>>/NM(fitz-A4)/Contents(RES-FIG7-36-01: Fig. 7 at 36 dB-Hz: the published curve, and what Gate A's 6 dB figure compares it with [Implemented, unverified, impact high]\nWe do: 0053 compares the published 0.29 with our Pd at Pfa 0.05. The 6 dB gap is the statement that our per-cell detector at 30 dB-Hz scores 0.281 at Pfa 0.05, "matching" the published 0.29 at 36. The write-up's table labels 0.29 as Pd at Pfa 0.05, and the bracket claim \("0.29 lies between 0.07 and 0.92"\) sets it against two values taken at Pfa 0.05.\nIf we got it wrong: At Pfa 0.7 our per-cell detector at 36 dB-Hz is near Pd 1 \(0.974 already at Pfa 0.2\). A weaker signal drives a detector toward Pd = Pfa, never below it, so no C/N0 shift turns our per-cell curve into theirs. The 6 dB number should be withdrawn, not refined. What the curve does tell us is about the false-alarm unit \(EVAL-CASE-01\). A cleaner place to measure a strength offset is the Sensors figure \(RES-SENSORS6-01\).\nTo close: Fix the write-up now: drop "6 dB worse" and the 36 dB-Hz containment, or restate both at the published Pfa. Then read our per-map Pd at Pfa 0.63 to 0.71 at 36 dB-Hz from the EVAL-CASE-01 scan: the only like-for-like comparison this curve allows.)/T(RES-FIG7-36-01)/BM/Multiply>>
endobj

