2164 0 obj
<</Type/Annot/Subtype/Highlight/C[.45 .6 .95]/P 1332 0 R/F 4/QuadPoints[222.43939 704.3906 446.39295 704.3906 222.43939 689.85726 446.39295 689.85726]/Rect[219.01374 688.9489 449.8186 705.29898]/AP<</N 2165 0 R>>/NM(fitz-A0)/Contents(REAL-SCALE-01: The scale of |CAF|^2 on real recordings [Paper silent, our choice, impact high]\nWe do: In simulation we standardize tiles with one scalar mean and standard deviation from the training set \(NET-INPUTNORM-01\). On real data the CAF scale follows the recording's int16 scaling and front-end gain, which differ between OAKBAT and TEXBAT and between recordings. M1's real-data runs avoided the question by training on real data: 0011 and 0014 take their mean and standard deviation from tiles of OAKBAT's clean recording. A model trained only on simulation has no such statistics.\nIf we got it wrong: The faithful model could call every real tile a peak, or none, for reasons that have nothing to do with spoofing. A fix that estimates the scale on the target recording is target-domain calibration and has to be labelled \(Hamza's feedback: the input-standardization row and technical correction 5\).\nTo close: Test: compare the noise floor of real and synthetic |CAF|^2 at matched C/N0, then fix one rule and state it: \(a\) scale each real map by its own noise floor, a per-map step the paper does not have but that uses no labels; or \(b\) estimate one gain per recording from its first clean seconds, which is target-calibrated. Report Pd and Pfa under the chosen rule, and the other as a check.)/T(REAL-SCALE-01)/BM/Multiply>>
endobj

