2188 0 obj
<</Type/Annot/Subtype/Highlight/C[.45 .6 .95]/P 3 0 R/F 4/QuadPoints[117.6378 393.0894 290.46775 393.0894 117.6378 378.556 290.46775 378.556]/Rect[114.212139 377.64768 293.8934 393.9977]/AP<</N 2189 0 R>>/NM(fitz-A0)/Contents(EVAL-CASE-01: What counts as one false alarm: one cell, or one whole map [Paper silent, our choice, impact high]\nWe do: Three readings, in three scripts. Per cell: one noise tile per scenario is one negative \(run.py\). Per map: the largest fused score over every noise cell of a stride-6 map, about 25,000 cells, is one negative \(run_fullscan.py\). A middle reading: the largest over 2000 random noise cells \(run_gatea_attribution.py:73\).\nIf we got it wrong: Gate A's containment claim rests on not choosing. Two measurements now point to whole maps. First, the journal's 36 dB-Hz curve has Pd below Pfa at every threshold, which a per-cell test of one score on noise tiles against peak tiles hardly ever produces, but a per-map test \(worst noise cell anywhere against the one peak cell\) produces naturally at low C/N0. Second, about 189 noise cases per C/N0 \(EVAL-TESTSET-01\) fits maps or sampled tiles, not all cells. The per-map value also depends on the map size: the paper's stride-1 map has 158,600 cells, six times our scan, which would push it lower still.\nTo close: Experiment: the stride-1 full-map scan, saving every fused score so Pd can be read at any Pfa, and scoring a detection two ways \(the peak cell beats the threshold; the peak cell is the map's maximum\). Compare per-map Pd with the published curves where they exist: Pfa 0.63 to 0.9 at 36 dB-Hz, 0.036 to 1 at 39.)/T(EVAL-CASE-01)/BM/Multiply>>
endobj

